<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Settlements over the safety of social netw=
orks are growing so large that Meta is reporting significant drags on its b=
usiness.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">In August, the biggest social media compan=
y in the world agreed to a $17bn settlement with dozens of state attorneys =
general to put claims it had endangered children to rest without admitting =
guilt.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">In a quarterly earnings report last week, =
issued the same afternoon as a judge ordered the company to pay the state o=
f New Mexico $25bn, Meta reported net income significantly below Wall Stree=
t analysts=E2=80=99 expectations because of a payment towards the settlemen=
t with attorneys general, this one $9.9bn.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The dent in Meta=E2=80=99s business is a s=
ign that child safety activists are succeeding. In the Los Angeles social m=
edia addiction lawsuit that took place earlier this year, lawyers for the p=
laintiff made it clear they=E2=80=99re intent on making it untenable for Me=
ta to operate the current version of Instagram. They want to render endange=
ring children, which they believe Meta is doing, too expensive to continue.=
 They hope to push the company to the point where business as usual is unte=
nable because of the financial risks of safety.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Activists can=E2=80=99t celebrate yet, tho=
ugh. Despite the hefty payments, Meta still reported a 26% increase in quar=
terly revenue up to $64.6bn.</p>
